  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSurface high pressure will keep conditions dry throughout most of \nthe weekend. Warmer air will round the top of the ridge over the \nWestern US and filter into the region this weekend. This will \nresult in temperatures at least a few degrees warmer than \nyesterday. Highs will be in the 80s today and Sunday. Despite \nthese warmer temperatures, dewpoints will be in the 50s to low \n60s, so it will feel fairly pleasant. Overnight lows this \nweekend will be in the 50s and 60s. Clear skies and light winds \nwill be favorable for patchy fog during the early morning hours.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nSurface high pressure will weaken Sunday, allowing for a couple of \npassing waves to support isolated to scattered showers from Sunday \nafternoon through Monday afternoon. Limited instability and weak \nshear may be enough to support isolated thunderstorms as well. Late \nMonday, the trough overhead begins to dig, opening the door for a \nlow to drop into the Northeast Tuesday. This will favor an \nactive pattern through Wednesday with multiple chances for \nrain showers and thunderstorms across the region. With limited \nshowers in the morning and the potential for some clearing, \nmodels are showing some instability each afternoon. However, \nwith weak flow aloft, shear is expected to be too weak to \nmaintain thunderstorms for any extended period of time. As of \nnow, severe chances look limited over the region and that seems \nto line up with SPC's thinking as we have been excluded from any\nof their extended outlooks. Wraparound moisture from this \nsystem will support additional showers through at least \nThursday, potentially into Thursday night. A weak ridge and \nsurface high pressure will build back into the region by the end\nof the week, bringing an end to the showers. \n\nWith this early week system, PWATs will be modest at 1.4 to 1.8\ninches, though some guidance show slightly higher values. If \nthe flow becomes oriented parallel with a passing frontal \nboundary on Tuesday, that would lead to training showers and \nstorms. With multiple consecutive dry days leading up to this \nsystem, hydro concerns are low at this time. Models vary greatly\nwith shower coverage throughout the event and where heavier \nrainfall may fall. Still, WPC does highlight the region in their\nERO, highlighting the training potential.\n\nTemperatures will generally be cooler than normal next week, \nespecially from Tuesday though the end of the week. With the ridge \nbuilding in on Friday, warming temperatures would be favored in time \nfor the weekend.",
